.TH SWAYKEYS 1 "2026-08-04" "swaykeys" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
swaykeys \- help sheet for every active sway key binding
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B swaykeys
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B swaykeys
reads the sway config the way sway reads it \(em following
.BR include ,
expanding
.B set
variables, tracking
.B mode
blocks \(em and lists the bindings that actually fire, grouped into sections.
.PP
On a terminal it opens an interactive sheet: type to filter, and the section
headings stay put.\& Piped, it prints markdown.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " \fIPATH\fR"
Read this config instead of locating the running one.\& Disables the IPC
cross\-check, and lets the tool run with no compositor at all.
.TP
.BR \-\-format " " auto | plain | md | json
Output format.\&
.B auto
(the default) is the aligned sheet on a terminal and markdown when piped.\&
Naming a format explicitly also turns the pager off.
.TP
.BR \-\-pager " " auto | always | never
Interactive sheet.\&
.B auto
opens it on a terminal when no explicit
.B \-\-format
was asked for.
.TP
.BR \-\-color " " auto | always | never
Colour.\&
.B auto
means on a terminal only, and is disabled by the
.B NO_COLOR
environment variable whatever its value.\& An explicit
.B always
overrides both: an option typed now beats a variable from a shell profile.
.TP
.BR \-2 ", " \-\-two\-column
Lay the sheet out in two columns.\& Sections are cut where the balance falls
and the heading is repeated, so long commands are clipped to fit.
.TP
.B \-\-desc
Show the comment above a binding instead of its command.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Also show bindings that never fire because another one wins the chord.\& They
are dimmed and annotated with what beats them.
.TP
.BR \-\-mode " \fINAME\fR"
Only bindings in this binding mode.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.SH KEYS
Inside the interactive sheet, keys follow
.BR fzf (1):
.TP
.I printable characters
Add to the filter.\&
.B q
is not quit \(em it goes into the filter.
.TP
.BR Up ", " Down ", " Ctrl\-p ", " Ctrl\-n
Move the cursor.
.TP
.BR PageUp ", " PageDown ", " Home ", " End
Move by a screen, or to either end.
.TP
.BR Backspace ", " Ctrl\-w ", " Ctrl\-u
Delete a character, a word, or the whole filter.
.TP
.BR Esc ", " Ctrl\-c ", " Enter
Leave.
.SH SECTIONS
.TP
.B Standard
Keyboard bindings in the default mode.
.TP
.I mode name
One section per binding mode, named and ordered as in the config.
.TP
.B Mouse
Button bindings, plus the drag and resize that
.B floating_modifier
binds without naming a button.
.TP
.B Touchpad
.B bindgesture
bindings.
.TP
.B Switches
.B bindswitch
bindings, such as the laptop lid.
.TP
.B Built\-in
Ctrl+Alt+F1..F12, which sway handles itself.\& It is a fallback: a config
binding on the same chord wins.
.SH WHICH BINDING FIRES
sway keeps bindings in five separate lists per mode, and a binding only ever
collides with, or is removed by, something in its own list.\& A
.B bindsym \-\-to\-code $mod+d
and a plain
.B bindsym $mod+d
therefore both survive; the first fires only because the keycode list is
queried first.\& By default only the winner is listed, and
.B \-\-all
shows the loser alongside it.
.PP
.B \-\-to\-code
is a request, not a guarantee: sway keeps the binding in the keycode list only
if every keysym in the chord maps to exactly one keycode.\& A keysym on two
keys \(em
.B Print
on a stock
.B us
layout, for instance \(em sends the whole binding back to the keysym list,
where it does collide with a plain
.BR bindsym .
.PP
Shadowing is only reported when both candidates would be equally applicable to
the same event.\& Where sway would choose using facts about the runtime input
device, nothing is claimed.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Lines beginning with
.B bind
or
.B unbind
that cannot be parsed are reported on standard error as
.BR "UNPARSED \fIfile\fR:\fIline\fR: \fItext\fR" .
A help sheet that silently drops a binding is worse than no help sheet.
.PP
Exit status is 0 unless the config could not be read.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SWAYSOCK
Path to the sway IPC socket, used to cross\-check the config that was found
against the one the running compositor loaded.\&
.B I3SOCK
is accepted as a fallback.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disables colour, whatever its value, unless
.B \-\-color always
is given.
.SH FILES
Without
.BR \-\-config ,
the root config is located in sway's own search order:
.PP
.PD 0
.IP 1. 4
.I ~/.sway/config
.IP 2. 4
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/sway/config
.IP 3. 4
.I ~/.i3/config
.IP 4. 4
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/i3/config
.IP 5. 4
.I /etc/sway/config
.IP 6. 4
.I /etc/i3/config
.PD
.PP
The IPC returns the root config verbatim, without expanding
.B include
\(em so the path is needed regardless, since includes resolve against the
directory of their parent.
.SH EXAMPLES
Browse every binding:
.RS
.EX
swaykeys
.EE
.RE
.PP
Drop a cheat sheet into a README:
.RS
.EX
swaykeys > BINDINGS.md
.EE
.RE
.PP
Two columns in a floating terminal, with descriptions from the config
comments:
.RS
.EX
foot \-a floating swaykeys \-2 \-\-desc \-\-pager never
.EE
.RE
.PP
Find out what shadows what:
.RS
.EX
swaykeys \-\-all \-\-format plain | grep shadowed
.EE
.RE
.PP
Read someone else's config, with no compositor running:
.RS
.EX
swaykeys \-\-config ./their\-sway\-config \-\-format md
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sway (1),
.BR sway (5),
.BR sway-input (5),
.BR sway-ipc (7),
.BR fzf (1)
